„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcbut all these worketh that one and the selfsame Spirit, dividing to every man severally as he will.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFor as the body is one, and hath many members, and all the members of that one body, being many, are one body: so also is Christ.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¡For by one Spirit are we all baptized into one body, whether we be Jews or Gentiles, whether we be bond or free; and have been all made to drink into one Spirit.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ)For the body is not one member, but many.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhIf the foot shall say, Because I am not the hand, I am not of the body; is it therefore not of the body?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjAnd if the ear shall say, Because I am not the eye, I am not of the body; is it therefore not of the body?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjIf the whole body were an eye, where were the hearing? If the whole were hearing, where were the smelling?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWBut now hath God set the members every one of them in the body, as it hath pleased him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ5And if they were all one member, where were the body?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ0But now are they many members, yet but one body.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙtAnd the eye cannot say unto the hand, I have no need of thee: nor again the head to the feet, I have no need of you.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVNay, much more those members of the body, which seem to be more feeble, are necessary:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¥and those members of the body, which we think to be less honourable, upon these we bestow more abundant honour; and our uncomely parts have more abundant comeliness.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙˆFor our comely parts have no need: but God hath tempered the body together, having given more abundant honour to that part which lacked:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙkthat there should be no schism in the body; but that the members should have the same care one for another.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙzAnd whether one member suffer, all the members suffer with it; or one member be honoured, all the members rejoice with it.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ9Now ye are the body of Christ, and members in particular.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙµAnd God hath set some in the church, first apostles, secondarily prophets, thirdly teachers, after that miracles, then gifts of healings, helps, governments, diversities of tongues.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRAre all apostles? are all prophets? are all teachers? are all workers of miracles?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙKHave all the gifts of healing? do all speak with tongues? do all interpret?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙQBut covet earnestly the best gifts: and yet shew I unto you a more excellent way.¦layout ªlast_verse